#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521905 :question/problem "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{16{t}^{2}+6},\\,4t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨\\sqrt{25{u}^{2}+6},\\,5u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-k\n$$ is false. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{5k}{4}\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ is true. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+6},\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521916 :question/problem "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1\n$$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=⟨3{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2},\\,t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 3.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-1\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1\n$$ is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(u)=⟨243{u}^{4}-18{u}^{2},\\,3u⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1.$” is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx=3{y}^{4}-2{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521917 :question/problem "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4\n$$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=⟨3{t}^{4}-4{t}^{2},\\,t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 4.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-4\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4\n$$ is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=16,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(u)=⟨768{u}^{4}-64{u}^{2},\\,4u⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1.$” is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx=3{y}^{4}-4{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521929 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\iint_D 3 y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(4,0)$, $(5,0)$, $(-3,4)$ and $(-4,4)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3 y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x + 2 y,\\qquad v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y)\\mapsto (u(x,y),v(x,y)).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T^{-1}(4,0)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,0) \\\\ T^{-1}(5,0)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(5\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(5\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(5,0) \\\\ T^{-1}(-3,4)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-3\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(-3\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(5,4) \\\\ T^{-1}(-4,4)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-4\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(-4\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):4\\le u\\le 5,\\ 0\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/31/31a73ea464a3b414e81849dbcd7b5a8361544eaa22a55b895ceb231ca2c1b42c.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\partial u/\\partial x&\\partial u/\\partial y\\\\\\partial v/\\partial x&\\partial v/\\partial y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving these affine equations also gives\n\n$$\nx=u - 2 v,\\qquad y=v.\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 3 y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_\\Delta 3 u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\iint_\\Delta 3 u v^{2}\\,\\left|1\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\int_{0}^{4}\\int_{4}^{5}u v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\int_{0}^{4}v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{4}^{5}u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\left[\\frac{v^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{4}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{4}^{5} \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)^{3}/3-\\left(\\left(0\\right)^{3}/3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)^{2}/2-\\left(\\left(4\\right)^{2}/2\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\frac{25}{2}\\right)-\\left(8\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right) \\\\ &=288.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521931 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^{2} \\left(3 x - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(\\frac{5}{3},1)$, $(\\frac{7}{3},1)$, $(\\frac{11}{3},3)$ and $(3,3)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=y^{2} \\left(3 x - 2 y\\right)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=3 x - 2 y,\\qquad v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y)\\mapsto (u(x,y),v(x,y)).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T^{-1}(\\frac{5}{3},1)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(3,1) \\\\ T^{-1}(\\frac{7}{3},1)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(5,1) \\\\ T^{-1}(\\frac{11}{3},3)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{11}{3}\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{11}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(5,3) \\\\ T^{-1}(3,3)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):3\\le u\\le 5,\\ 1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f9/f9dc752c5aeff34bc911428090212f09e0b3322041842726bd53dd7185563e7d.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\partial u/\\partial x&\\partial u/\\partial y\\\\\\partial v/\\partial x&\\partial v/\\partial y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}3&-2\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving these affine equations also gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u}{3} + \\frac{2 v}{3},\\qquad y=v.\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D y^{2} \\left(3 x - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{1}{3}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{3}^{5}u v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int_{1}^{3}v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{3}^{5}u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{v^{3}}{3}\\right]_{1}^{3}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{3}^{5} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\left(3\\right)^{3}/3-\\left(\\left(1\\right)^{3}/3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)^{2}/2-\\left(\\left(3\\right)^{2}/2\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\left(9\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\frac{25}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{26}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(8\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{208}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521936 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\iint_D 3 y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(2,0)$, $(4,0)$, $(-4,4)$ and $(-6,4)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3 y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x + 2 y,\\qquad v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y)\\mapsto (u(x,y),v(x,y)).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T^{-1}(2,0)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(2,0) \\\\ T^{-1}(4,0)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,0) \\\\ T^{-1}(-4,4)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-4\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(-4\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,4) \\\\ T^{-1}(-6,4)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-6\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(-6\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(2,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):2\\le u\\le 4,\\ 0\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/2f/2f38ba39afcd37730bd37cbaca5b8a37feae8aa84f6f79be32a548aed4048482.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\partial u/\\partial x&\\partial u/\\partial y\\\\\\partial v/\\partial x&\\partial v/\\partial y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving these affine equations also gives\n\n$$\nx=u - 2 v,\\qquad y=v.\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 3 y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_\\Delta 3 u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\iint_\\Delta 3 u v^{2}\\,\\left|1\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\int_{0}^{4}\\int_{2}^{4}u v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\int_{0}^{4}v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{2}^{4}u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\left[\\frac{v^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{4}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{2}^{4} \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)^{3}/3-\\left(\\left(0\\right)^{3}/3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)^{2}/2-\\left(\\left(2\\right)^{2}/2\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(8\\right)-\\left(2\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\right) \\\\ &=384.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521942 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(2,1)$, $(4,1)$, $(-2,4)$ and $(-4,4)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x + 2 y,\\qquad v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y)\\mapsto (u(x,y),v(x,y)).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T^{-1}(2,1)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,1) \\\\ T^{-1}(4,1)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(6,1) \\\\ T^{-1}(-2,4)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-2\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(-2\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(6,4) \\\\ T^{-1}(-4,4)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-4\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(-4\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):4\\le u\\le 6,\\ 1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/64/642ee19399f1c37417b256bffc49b4b1458b83b7ea9d1f758e18cac7f1fa620f.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\partial u/\\partial x&\\partial u/\\partial y\\\\\\partial v/\\partial x&\\partial v/\\partial y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving these affine equations also gives\n\n$$\nx=u - 2 v,\\qquad y=v.\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2}\\,\\left|1\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=1\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{4}^{6}u v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=1\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{4}^{6}u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=1\\cdot \\left[\\frac{v^{3}}{3}\\right]_{1}^{4}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{4}^{6} \\\\ &=1\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)^{3}/3-\\left(\\left(1\\right)^{3}/3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(6\\right)^{2}/2-\\left(\\left(4\\right)^{2}/2\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=1\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(18\\right)-\\left(8\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=1\\cdot \\left(21\\right)\\cdot \\left(10\\right) \\\\ &=210.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521948 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^{2} \\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(- \\frac{1}{3},1)$, $(\\frac{2}{3},1)$, $(- \\frac{4}{3},4)$ and $(- \\frac{7}{3},4)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=y^{2} \\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=3 x + 2 y,\\qquad v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y)\\mapsto (u(x,y),v(x,y)).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T^{-1}(- \\frac{1}{3},1)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(1,1) \\\\ T^{-1}(\\frac{2}{3},1)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,1) \\\\ T^{-1}(- \\frac{4}{3},4)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{4}{3}\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{4}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,4) \\\\ T^{-1}(- \\frac{7}{3},4)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{7}{3}\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{7}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(1,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\le u\\le 4,\\ 1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/35/35d966f8a5a8e6e04353e202788230edbe6581fa7820e04fbe86406285c2194d.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\partial u/\\partial x&\\partial u/\\partial y\\\\\\partial v/\\partial x&\\partial v/\\partial y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}3&2\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving these affine equations also gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u}{3} - \\frac{2 v}{3},\\qquad y=v.\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D y^{2} \\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{1}{3}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{4}u v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{v^{3}}{3}\\right]_{1}^{4}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{1}^{4} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)^{3}/3-\\left(\\left(1\\right)^{3}/3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)^{2}/2-\\left(\\left(1\\right)^{2}/2\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(8\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(21\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{15}{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{105}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521978 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the level surface $\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right)=0$ at the point $(0,3,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,3,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,3,-3).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,3,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)&={f}_{x}(x,y,z)i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)k\\\\&=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))k\\\\&=\\left(2 x + 2\\right)i+\\left(4 y - 8\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\∇f(0,3,-3)&=\\left(2\\right)i+\\left(4\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\&=⟨2,4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,3,-3)$ and passes through the point $(0,3,-3)$ can be written as\n\n$$r=⟨0,3,-3⟩+t\\,∇f(0,3,-3)=⟨0,3,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,1⟩.$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521982 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the level surface $\\left(-\\left(x - 1\\right)\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 2\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(z - 2\\right)=0$ at the point $(0,-3,5).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\left(-\\left(x - 1\\right)\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 2\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(z - 2\\right).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,-3,5)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,-3,5).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,-3,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)&={f}_{x}(x,y,z)i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)k\\\\&=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\left(-\\left(x - 1\\right)\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 2\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(z - 2\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\left(-\\left(x - 1\\right)\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 2\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(z - 2\\right))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\left(-\\left(x - 1\\right)\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 2\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(z - 2\\right))k\\\\&=\\left(2 x - 2\\right)i+\\left(4 y + 8\\right)j+\\left(-1\\right)k\\\\∇f(0,-3,5)&=\\left(-2\\right)i+\\left(-4\\right)j+\\left(-1\\right)k\\\\&=⟨-2,-4,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,-3,5)$ and passes through the point $(0,-3,5)$ can be written as\n\n$$r=⟨0,-3,5⟩+t\\,∇f(0,-3,5)=⟨0,-3,5⟩+t\\,⟨-2,-4,-1⟩.$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521987 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the level surface $\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 1\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right)=0$ at the point $(0,-2,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 1\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,-2,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,-2,-3).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,-2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)&={f}_{x}(x,y,z)i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)k\\\\&=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 1\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 1\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 1\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))k\\\\&=\\left(2 x + 2\\right)i+\\left(4 y + 4\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\∇f(0,-2,-3)&=\\left(2\\right)i+\\left(-4\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\&=⟨2,-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,-2,-3)$ and passes through the point $(0,-2,-3)$ can be written as\n\n$$r=⟨0,-2,-3⟩+t\\,∇f(0,-2,-3)=⟨0,-2,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,-4,1⟩.$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521995 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the level surface $x^{2}+2\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right)=0$ at the point $(1,2,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=x^{2}+2\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,2,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,2,-3).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)&={f}_{x}(x,y,z)i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)k\\\\&=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}+2\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}+2\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2}+2\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))k\\\\&=\\left(2 x\\right)i+\\left(4 y - 4\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\∇f(1,2,-3)&=\\left(2\\right)i+\\left(4\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\&=⟨2,4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,2,-3)$ and passes through the point $(1,2,-3)$ can be written as\n\n$$r=⟨1,2,-3⟩+t\\,∇f(1,2,-3)=⟨1,2,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,1⟩.$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521997 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the level surface $\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2y^{2}-\\left(z + 2\\right)=0$ at the point $(0,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2y^{2}-\\left(z + 2\\right).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1,1).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)&={f}_{x}(x,y,z)i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)k\\\\&=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2y^{2}-\\left(z + 2\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2y^{2}-\\left(z + 2\\right))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2y^{2}-\\left(z + 2\\right))k\\\\&=\\left(2 x + 2\\right)i+\\left(4 y\\right)j+\\left(-1\\right)k\\\\∇f(0,1,1)&=\\left(2\\right)i+\\left(4\\right)j+\\left(-1\\right)k\\\\&=⟨2,4,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,1,1)$ and passes through the point $(0,1,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$r=⟨0,1,1⟩+t\\,∇f(0,1,1)=⟨0,1,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,-1⟩.$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522753 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\frac{1}{x}\\le y\\le \\frac{2}{x},\\,\\frac{1}{xy}\\le z\\le \\frac{2}{xy}\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=xy,\\,w=xyz,\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\,{e}^{xyz}\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$ \n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=xy,\\,w=xyz\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ yz & xz & xy\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =xy\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =xy(x-0) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y \\\\  & =uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{uv}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{e}^{xyz}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{e}^{w}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{uv}|\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{{e}^{w}}{uv}\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{w}}{uv}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522774 :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=11\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522780 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=3x+2z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{12}{1+(3x+2z)^{2}}\\,dV,\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\le 2x+y\\le \\frac{2}{\\pi },\\,\\,0\\le 3x+2z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=3x+2z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\le u\\le \\frac{2}{\\pi },\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{12}{1+(3x+2z)^{2}}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{12}{1+{v}^{2}}\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{4}{(1+{v}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/\\pi }^{2/\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{4}{(1+{v}^{2})}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/\\pi }^{2/\\pi }\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{(1+{v}^{2})}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{1/\\pi }^{2/\\pi }\\cdot {[4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡v]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{\\pi })\\cdot (4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522782 :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=9\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+10\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522798 :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522803 :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522808 :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=9\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522809 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\le 2,\\,2\\le x+y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,v=x+y,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(x+y)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$ \n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,v=x+y,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps: \n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & -2y & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2x & -2y \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2x+2y \\\\  & =2(x+y) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$ \n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+y)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{sinh}⁡u\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡u}{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡u}{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522901 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=3t e^{3t},\\quad y=5(t^2+2)e^{-3t},\\quad -\\infty<t<\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t$ for $t\\ne - \\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t\\right)\\right)e^{3t}+3t\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{3t}\\right)\\right),\\quad y'(t)=5\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t^2+2\\right)\\right)e^{-3t}+5(t^2+2)\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{-3t}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3e^{3t}+ 3t(3e^{3t}),\\quad y'(t)=5(2t)e^{-3t}+5(t^2+2)(-3e^{-3t})\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3(1+3t)e^{3t},\\quad y'(t)=5(2t-3(t^2+2))e^{-3t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5(2t-3(t^2+2))e^{-3t}}{3(1+3t)e^{3t}}=\\frac{5(2t-3(t^2+2))}{3(1+3t)}e^{-6t}.\n$$\n\nThis derivative is defined for $t\\ne - \\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522908 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1t}{t+1},\\quad y=\\frac{1t^2}{t+4},\\quad t>0,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{1\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t\\right)\\right)(t+1)-1t\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t+1\\right)\\right)}{(t+1)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{1\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t^2\\right)\\right)(t+4)-1t^2\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t+4\\right)\\right)}{(t+4)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{1(t+1)-1t(1)}{(t+1)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{2t(t+4)-1t^2(1)}{(t+4)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{1t+1-1t}{(t+1)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{2t^2+8t-1t^2}{(t+4)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{1t^2+8t}{(t+4)^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\frac{1t^2+8t}{(t+4)^2}}{\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2}}=\\frac{(1t^2+8t)(t+1)^2}{1(t+4)^2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522915 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=3t e^{1t},\\quad y=5(t^2+2)e^{-1t},\\quad -\\infty<t<\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t$ for $t\\ne -1.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t\\right)\\right)e^{1t}+3t\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{1t}\\right)\\right),\\quad y'(t)=5\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t^2+2\\right)\\right)e^{-1t}+5(t^2+2)\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{-1t}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3e^{1t}+ 3t(1e^{1t}),\\quad y'(t)=5(2t)e^{-1t}+5(t^2+2)(-1e^{-1t})\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3(1+1t)e^{1t},\\quad y'(t)=5(2t-1(t^2+2))e^{-1t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5(2t-1(t^2+2))e^{-1t}}{3(1+1t)e^{1t}}=\\frac{5(2t-1(t^2+2))}{3(1+1t)}e^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nThis derivative is defined for $t\\ne -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522916 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=2t e^{1t},\\quad y=5(t^2+2)e^{-1t},\\quad -\\infty<t<\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t$ for $t\\ne -1.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t\\right)\\right)e^{1t}+2t\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{1t}\\right)\\right),\\quad y'(t)=5\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t^2+2\\right)\\right)e^{-1t}+5(t^2+2)\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{-1t}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2e^{1t}+ 2t(1e^{1t}),\\quad y'(t)=5(2t)e^{-1t}+5(t^2+2)(-1e^{-1t})\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2(1+1t)e^{1t},\\quad y'(t)=5(2t-1(t^2+2))e^{-1t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5(2t-1(t^2+2))e^{-1t}}{2(1+1t)e^{1t}}=\\frac{5(2t-1(t^2+2))}{2(1+1t)}e^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nThis derivative is defined for $t\\ne -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522919 :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0a61578b1ef5383e5e8e9e23c3c6309ede794d6b4f9c47a7ce8103b02f79fe2.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=6\\left(2t{e}^{t}\\right),\\,y=4\\left({t}^{2}+2t\\right),\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6\\left(2{e}^{t}(t+1)\\right),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4\\left(2t+2\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4\\left(2t+2\\right)}{6\\left(2{e}^{t}(t+1)\\right)}=\\frac{4\\left(1\\right)}{6\\left({e}^{t}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{4\\left(1\\right)}{6\\left({e}^{1}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\left(1\\right)}{6\\left(e\\right)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522920 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{4t}{t+2},\\quad y=\\frac{4t^2}{t+1},\\quad t>0,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{4\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t\\right)\\right)(t+2)-4t\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t+2\\right)\\right)}{(t+2)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{4\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t^2\\right)\\right)(t+1)-4t^2\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t+1\\right)\\right)}{(t+1)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{4(t+2)-4t(1)}{(t+2)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{8t(t+1)-4t^2(1)}{(t+1)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{4t+8-4t}{(t+2)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{8t^2+8t-4t^2}{(t+1)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{8}{(t+2)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{4t^2+8t}{(t+1)^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\frac{4t^2+8t}{(t+1)^2}}{\\frac{8}{(t+2)^2}}=\\frac{(4t^2+8t)(t+2)^2}{8(t+1)^2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522923 :question/problem "![](images/19/19689860cee70dfdb45a3d2f6708bcdab42b82b3ea20ea352ee0b2060fe7be13.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨7\\sin(t),\\,7\\cos(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\nr'(t)=⟨7\\cos(t),\\,-7\\sin(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}7\\cos(t)\\cdot 7\\cos(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}49\\cos^{2}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522925 :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0ac24d590f74a62bd45885b90190b82018372fd086d8cdda6ee8fde115d6fdd.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨6{e}^{\\sin(t)},\\,2{e}^{\\cos(t)}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨6\\cos(t)\\,{e}^{\\sin(t)},\\,-2\\sin(t)\\,{e}^{\\cos(t)}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2{e}^{\\cos(t)}\\cdot 6\\cos(t)\\,{e}^{\\sin(t)}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }12\\cos(t)\\,{e}^{\\cos(t)+\\sin(t)}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522926 :question/problem "![](images/3f/3fc4346f3603d6d844584973c30b8ecf1e738a4d0a393ed1ab3ec62f764596cc.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨12\\sin(t),\\,12\\cos(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\nr'(t)=⟨12\\cos(t),\\,-12\\sin(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}12\\cos(t)\\cdot 12\\cos(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}144\\cos^{2}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522927 :question/problem "![](images/70/70ef514109413878d6cafb2931b51ee1d1b48eca053d4c085f0ebe5e8170aa3e.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\sin(t),\\,6\\sin(2t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨2\\cos(t),\\,12\\cos(2t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-2\\sin(t))\\cdot 12\\cos(2t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-24\\sin(t)\\cos(2t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522929 :question/problem "![](images/10/10458df45838335a3856d9758930925eea52ed6a59fb5791edab3cccff6c3e2d.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨17\\sin(t),\\,17\\cos(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\nr'(t)=⟨17\\cos(t),\\,-17\\sin(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}17\\cos(t)\\cdot 17\\cos(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}289\\cos^{2}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522930 :question/problem "![](images/ef/efb35e6552df72236d914018d36b1e582611a91de649cbe69c182b0469e5c15d.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\cos(t),\\,12\\sin(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-2\\sin(t),\\,12\\cos(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\cos(t)\\cdot 12\\cos(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }24\\cos^{2}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522934 :question/problem "![](images/7e/7ee7d04b032a332f381056a91fb658904268631b0f520a54787b234f0ccd67a1.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨13\\sin(t),\\,13\\cos(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\nr'(t)=⟨13\\cos(t),\\,-13\\sin(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}13\\cos(t)\\cdot 13\\cos(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}169\\cos^{2}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522939 :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2158b3d10187977f243ca6ba67223d722fadeca0a567430595416b94d044873.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨4\\cos(t),\\,2\\sin(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-4\\sin(t),\\,2\\cos(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\cos(t)\\cdot 2\\cos(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\cos^{2}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522941 :question/problem "![](images/46/46f114546d9d9fe080d32aef108e75894e9b67b9883637b17595aa8a374eac74.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨18\\sin(t),\\,18\\cos(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\nr'(t)=⟨18\\cos(t),\\,-18\\sin(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}18\\cos(t)\\cdot 18\\cos(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}324\\cos^{2}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522954 :question/problem "![](images/94/948754761eebc091cffa3aade21d37c92dfa38d69df076bcbb59ee03a06330cf.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\sin(2t),\\,6\\cos(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, piecewise smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨4\\cos(2t),\\,-6\\sin(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\cos(t)\\cdot 4\\cos(2t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}24\\cos(t)\\cos(2t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522969 :question/problem "![](images/61/611311e6976285f7e656ae16812999e6abd30184a5823abe0bb9d7cb4290d22e.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\sin(t),\\,6\\cos(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨2\\cos(t),\\,-6\\sin(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\cos(t)\\cdot 2\\cos(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }12\\cos^{2}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522980 :question/problem "![](images/8a/8ab774269b76212bc10b4364104217fe24209cf6f3fdf910cba6a63ff1876a49.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨10\\cos(t),\\,4\\sin(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-10\\sin(t),\\,4\\cos(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }10\\cos(t)\\cdot 4\\cos(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }40\\cos^{2}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522984 :question/problem "![](images/de/de9f562dd470e2377e39a7f604b34ca044a4da158ddb0877757b65387c3e2003.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨4(\\sin(t)-\\sin(2t)),\\,3(\\cos(t)-\\cos(2t))⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨4(\\cos(t)-2\\cos(2t)),\\,3(-\\sin(t)+2\\sin(2t))⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}4(-\\sin(t)+\\sin(2t))\\cdot 3(-\\sin(t)+2\\sin(2t))\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}12(\\sin(2t)-\\sin(t))(2\\sin(2t)-\\sin(t))\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
